    "narrative": "The heavy lifters for Ghost Ride the WHIP tonight are led by **James Wood**, who commands the highest score of the slate at 76.06. His matchup against G. Holmes is favorable, with an xERA of 4.71 acting as a green light for his elite .423 xwOBA. Given the premium we place on speed and counting stats, **James Wood** hitting out of the leadoff spot is an optimal setup for potential multi-run contributions. While **Tyler Soderstrom** sits behind him in the confidence rankings (67.73), he remains a strong start despite facing a respectable Sonny Gray; his score is buoyed significantly by a +10% park factor and the stability of a clean-up role. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** (66.05) is locked into the UTIL slot, leveraging a solid platoon edge against Roki Sasaki that keeps his efficiency well above our 60-point threshold for mandatory starts.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involves the bottom end of our OF and middle infield slots. **Seiya Suzuki** (58.84) narrowly held off the bench, surviving a marginal score by capitalizing on the slow delivery of Pallante to hunt stolen bases\u2014our primary scarcity driver. **Cole Young** (55.02) also skates by; while his matchup against the elite Roki Sasaki is treacherous, his lead-off placement and a 5% wind-out boost keep him in the lineup over speculative bench options. **Henry Bolte** (48.75) and **Dalton Rushing** (44.75) represent our \"marginal\" tier; they remain active only to protect game caps and mitigate the void left by **Vinnie Pasquantino**\u2019s IL stint and the lack of production from depth pieces like **Jose Caballero**, who failed to crack the starting rotation today. Managers should note that **Dalton Rushing** and **Cole Young** are still pending official lineup confirmation; any drop in their batting order could immediately slide them below the 40-point efficiency floor, warranting a late-minute check on whether to bench them to preserve our remaining caps for a more favorable series.",
